<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That Is a Circle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures and simple words to describe shapes to classmates (e.g., using hands to represent the shape of a circle). Can use a combination of drawings and words to clarify their responses about shapes (e.g., drawing the shape of a triangle and writing the word next to it). Can describe shapes using body language and pointing during classroom activities (e.g., miming a square shape by using the arms to create straight lines).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Guess the Shape!" The teacher uses TPR and gestures to mime a shape (e.g., making a circle with arms, outlining a square with hands). Students guess the shape name in English. This activates prior knowledge of shapes and introduces non-verbal communication for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing shapes using simple sentences, gestures, and drawing. For example, the teacher draws a circle on the board and says, "This is a circle. It is round." while making a round gesture. Then, draws a square and says, "This is a square. Look, four sides." while using fingers to count the sides. This models how to use words and non-verbal cues to clarify meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visuals and gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a drawing of a circle): "Is this a square or a circle?" (Students say "circle" or point to the circle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Making a triangle shape with hands): "Can you show me a triangle with your hands?" (Students imitate the gesture).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture cards of everyday objects that represent shapes (e.g., a round clock, a square window, a triangular slice of pizza, a rectangular door). A simple repetitive chant or song about shapes. Flashcards with shape names (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes and describing their characteristics using simple phrases and demonstrating understanding through gestures and pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays a picture card (e.g., the round clock). "What shape is it?" Students respond chorally "A circle!" The teacher models "It is a circle. It is round." (using a round gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a shape flashcard (e.g., triangle) and asks, "Can you find something that is a triangle?" Students point to an object in the classroom or a picture on the board that has a triangular shape.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Singing a "Shapes Song": Teacher leads a simple song where students use gestures to represent each shape as its name is sung (e.g., "A circle, a circle, it's round and round!" while making a circle with hands).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using "What shape is it? That is a [shape]." and "It is [adjective]." They apply vocabulary (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star, round, big, small) and grammar (Shape + noun, WH-questions, Present simple) with an emphasis on using gestures, body language, and simple drawings to clarify their communication (Mediation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Teacher Says, Student Draws/Shows."</b> The teacher calls out a shape name (e.g., "Draw a rectangle!"). Students draw the shape on mini-whiteboards or in their notebooks. Then, the teacher says, "Show me a star with your hands!" Students use their body to mime the shape. The teacher circulates, providing individual feedback on clarity of drawing and gesture, reinforcing the pronunciation of /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Describe and Guess."</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a shape. Student A comes to the front, sees the shape, and describes it to the class using a simple word (e.g., "It is round.") and a gesture (making a circular motion with their finger). Other students guess the shape ("A circle!"). The teacher guides students to add more details with gestures, like "It is big!" (spreading arms wide).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check: "Mediator Match."</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of shape flashcards and a set of simple object picture cards (e.g., a picture of a wheel, a window). One student picks an object card and describes its primary shape using words and gestures (e.g., "Look! It is a circle." while drawing a circle in the air). The partner listens, looks, and tries to find the matching shape flashcard. They take turns mediating the description and identifying the shape.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Shape Sorting Activity Presentation." Students will work in small groups to present their previously sorted shapes (from the 21st Century Skills Project) to their classmates, using gestures, simple words, and drawings to explain their categorizations and clarify the shapes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students are seated in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has their "Shape Sorting Activity" materials (pre-cut shapes or shape drawings sorted into categories like "circles," "squares," etc.). One student from the group points to a pile of sorted shapes (e.g., all the circles). This student then uses words ("These are circles.") and gestures (tracing a circle in the air, or using hands to show the roundness) to describe them to their group members. If a classmate looks confused, the student can draw a quick circle on a small piece of paper or point to a round object in the classroom (e.g., "Look! A clock is a circle!"). Group members can ask "What shape is it?" and the presenting student clarifies using words and demonstrations. Each student in the group gets a turn to present a different sorted shape category, focusing on clarifying for others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This task focuses on students' ability to use their shape vocabulary and grammar in a communicative context, specifically employing mediation skills (gestures, demonstrations, simple words, pointing, drawing) to ensure their classmates understand the shapes they are presenting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Shape Sorting Activity Presentation" and individual quick checks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will individually select one sorted shape from their group's project and explain it to the teacher or a peer. They must use at least one simple word ("This is a square.") and at least one gesture (miming the shape with their hands) or a quick drawing on the board/paper to describe and clarify the shape.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses gestures and/or body language to describe a shape clearly (e.g., miming a square, making a round motion for a circle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple words or phrases to name/describe the shape (e.g., "This is a circle," "It is round," "four sides").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to draw or point to a visual aid to clarify their response about the shape.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of shape names is intelligible (/s/ in "square," /r/ in "rectangle").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw their favorite shape from the sorting activity on an exit ticket. Next to it, they try to write its name.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They complete the sentence (or point to an emoji): "I can explain a [shape] with my hands and words: (Happy face / OK face / Sad face)."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you when you explained a shape using your hands?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard for you when you tried to help your friend understand a shape?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., students' use of gestures, their ability to describe shapes using simple words, clarity in drawings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks? (e.g., more practice with specific vocabulary, more structured sentence frames for description, clearer modeling of drawing to clarify).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully demonstrate the learning outcomes for mediation? How effective was the "Shape Sorting Activity Presentation" in fostering mediation skills?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Shapes Around Us" theme. The next thematic unit will build on descriptive language and early literacy skills, encouraging students to use mediation strategies (gestures, drawings) in new contexts, like describing family members or daily routines.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>